course: dictionary
lesson: 15345
title: A formal word for a serious ceremony
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Solemnisation is a long, stiff word, but you can break it into parts. Solemn means serious and dignified, and -isation turns it into an action. So solemnisation is the act of doing something in a serious, official way.
explain: The word is used mostly for weddings: «The solemnisation of their marriage took place in the old church.» The clues are the old church and the official feel of the sentence. People in everyday talk just say «the wedding», but a formal text chooses the longer word.
explain: Try the swap check. «The wedding of their marriage took place in the old church» sounds odd, so the sentence needs a word for the ceremony itself. Try «the ceremony of their marriage» and then «the solemnisation of their marriage» to see that they match in meaning.
fact: Solemnisation is a noun, so it names an action or an event.
fact: «Solemnisation» means the act of performing a ceremony, especially a wedding, in a serious and official way.
fact: «Solemn» means serious and dignified, so to solemnise something is to make it official with a solemn ceremony.
fact: British spelling is «solemnisation» with an s; US spelling is «solemnization» with a z.
fact: Legal and exam texts use this word to show that something was done formally and officially.
exercise: «The solemnisation of their marriage took place in the old church.» What does solemnisation mean? | A quick look at a map | *The act of performing a serious, official ceremony | A kind of soup | A loud street party with music
exercise: Ella says solemnisation is just another word for a party. Is she right? | *No, it is a serious, official ceremony | No, it means a school sports day | Yes, it means a noisy party with music | Yes, it means a picnic on the beach
exercise: Why would a legal text use the word «solemnisation»? | *To show that something was done formally and officially | To say the room was cold | To make the text shorter | To show a joke was told
test: What is a simple everyday way to say «the solemnisation of a marriage»? | A party | Moving house | Going shopping | *Getting married
test: Which sentence uses solemnisation correctly? | I ate a sandwich at the solemnisation of lunch | The solemnisation of the football match was fun because we kicked a ball | *The solemnisation of their marriage took place in the church | Solemnisation is a bright colour of paint
exam: Solemnisation is which part of speech? | *Noun | Verb | Adverb | Adjective
